Fillets and chamfers: two blends that take away the faces at a concave
edge and meet at its far end (mitcad#1106).

The case: a tray whose walls are as high as the bevel or rounding of their
inner top edges (2 high, a chamfer or fillet of 2 on the edges along the
top). Each blend takes its wall's inner face away, its contact on that face
running along the floor's edge. At a corner of the pocket the two blends
meet: PerformTwoCornerbyInter takes the pocket's vertical concave edge as
the pivot, and both blends' common points on it lie at its lower end, the
floor's corner, a vertex of the shape (ChFiDS_CommonPoint::IsVertex). The
data structure holds such a point as that vertex, a shape
(ChFi3d_IndexPointInDS), but the corner then set the tolerance of the
point with that index, as of a point of its own, and
TopOpeBRepDS_DataStructure::Point threw: the corner failed, and so did the
whole dressup wherever two such walls meet. A straight wall without
corners built.

- PerformTwoCornerbyInter: the tolerance of the common point on the pivot
  is set only where it is not a vertex, in both branches, as the other
  corners and the stripe ends do (a vertex keeps its own tolerance).

The trays' bevels and roundings now build at their size: the walls' top
and inner faces gone, the blends meeting the floor and, on walls as thick
as the blend, the outer walls, at one edge each, and the bodies' volumes
those of the exact shapes.

Upstream: new.
